 from Lithuanian paršiùkas. By surface analysis, Lithuanian par̃šas + -uk. Compare Belarusian парсю́к (parsjúk)."},{"parola":"rojst","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"prestito","incerta":"no","significato":"swampy place, overgrown with mosses, small bushes and other small vegetation","termine_origine":"raistas","catena":[{"codice":"lt","nome":"Lithuanian","termine":"raistas","categoria":"prestito"}],"lontana":"lt","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Borrowed from Lithuanian raistas."},{"parola":"siermięga","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"Unclear","significato":"sukmana (type of traditional coat once worn by peasants in Poland)","termine_origine":"šìrmas","catena":[{"codice":"lt","nome":"Lithuanian","termine":"šìrmas","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"lt","ambiti":["moda"],"via":"","testo":"Unclear. The exact cognates are found in Belarusian сярмя́га (sjarmjáha), Russian сермя́га (sermjága) and Ukrainian серм'я́га (sermʺjáha). Brückner proposes a derivation from Lithuanian ši̇̀rmas (“ashen, grey”)."},{"parola":"skierdź","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"prestito","incerta":"no","significato":"synonym of pasterz (“herdsman”)","termine_origine":"kerdžius","catena":[{"codice":"lt","nome":"Lithuanian","termine":"kerdžius","categoria":"prestito"},{"codice":"ine-pro","nome":"Proto-Indo-European","termine":"*(s)ḱerdʰ-","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"ine-pro","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Borrowed from Lithuanian (s)kerdžius, from Proto-Indo-European *(s)ḱerdʰ- (“to proceed, arrange”). Doublet of kierdel."},{"parola":"sterta","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"prestito","incerta":"no","significato":"stack, pile","termine_origine":"stìrta","catena":[{"codice":"lt","nome":"Lithuanian","termine":"stìrta","categoria":"prestito"}],"lontana":"lt","ambiti":["scienza","informatica"],"via":"","testo":"Borrowed from Lithuanian sti̇̀rta (“hay stack”)."},{"parola":"tekszla","pos":"noun","n_et